      "id": 1,
      "text": "New Houlka sprang to life in 1904 when the Gulf and Ship Island Railroad built a line just a mile east of the original Houlka.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Houlka%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 2004, the Gulf & Ship Island line through New Houlka was abandoned, ending a century of freight service.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Houlka%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The name 'Houlka' comes from Chickasaw, echoing the area’s long history as a Chickasaw village along the Natchez Trace.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/new_houlka_mississippi",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Old Houlka, the original settlement, dates back to a fur trading post before 1794, making it the oldest town in northern Mississippi.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Houlka%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 19th century, cotton plantations flourished around New Houlka, turning the town into a bustling market center.",
